\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Simulating Figure 5: The Field Near the Rod Tip During a Switching Impulse
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Bampaliaris Nikolaos
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
15-10-2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Summary
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure 5 of the Wang & Haiting paper shows the axial field near the rod tip at several instants of a switching impulse: (a) the applied field alone and (b) the field with the space charge of the first corona streamer. We reproduce it by driving the existing solver with a double-exponential voltage 
\begin_inset Formula $V(t)$
\end_inset
, creating the space charge of Figure 3 at the inception instant, and letting it drift under the rising applied field. The simulated field in the streamer region at inception lies between 
\begin_inset Formula $4.06$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $4.97\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
, in agreement with the paper's “4 to 5.5 kV/cm”. The simulation also shows that the field at the tip recovers to its inception value 
\begin_inset Formula $1.58\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
 after the first streamer, at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=3.25\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
, so a second streamer would incept within the time window of Figure 5.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
What the Paper Gives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure 5(b) is calculated “with the same condition as Figure 4”, i.e. the conditions of Figures 3 and 4: a rod-plane air gap of 
\begin_inset Formula $80\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
, rod tip radius 
\begin_inset Formula $0.1\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
, “a switching surge having the crest value of 320 kV with the time of wavefront of 100 µs”, first-streamer inception at 
\begin_inset Formula $27.5\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
, and streamer length 
\begin_inset Formula $2.7\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
. The figure plots 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset
 against 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset
 (
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $4\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
) and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
 (
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $4\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
); its field axis is cut at 
\begin_inset Formula $25\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 in (a) and 
\begin_inset Formula $15\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 in (b). The text states that when the first streamer incepts, the field in the streamer region is “of the range from 4 to 5.5 kV/cm”, in good agreement with the experimental value of 
\begin_inset Formula $5.2\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Not given: the tail of the impulse, the cone angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
, the time steps of the figure, and what happens after the first streamer (whether a second one is included).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
How the Simulation Works
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Impulse voltage.
\series default
 The double exponential 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V(t)=V_{p}\,k\left(e^{-\alpha t}-e^{-\beta t}\right)\label{eq:impulse}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

 is fitted so that it reaches exactly the crest 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{p}$
\end_inset
 at the time to crest 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{cr}$
\end_inset
 and falls to 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{p}/2$
\end_inset
 at the time to half value 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{h}$
\end_inset
. The crest time follows from 
\begin_inset Formula $dV/dt=0$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{cr}=\ln(\beta/\alpha)/(\beta-\alpha)$
\end_inset
, and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 normalises the crest, 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1/\left(e^{-\alpha T_{cr}}-e^{-\beta T_{cr}}\right)$
\end_inset
. The two conditions are solved numerically for 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset
. For 
\begin_inset Formula $320\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $100/2500\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
 this gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=291.1\,\text{s}^{-1}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=5.218\times10^{4}\,\text{s}^{-1}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1.0353$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Inception instant.
\series default
 The first streamer starts at 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{inc}$
\end_inset
, where 
\begin_inset Formula $V(t_{inc})=27.5\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
. With the parameters above, 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{inc}=1.671\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Space charge at inception.
\series default
 At 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{inc}$
\end_inset
 the cloud of Figure 3 is created: 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
 is solved so that the total field at the streamer tip equals 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}=5\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $V=27.5\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
, giving 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}=93.39^{\circ}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{cs}=11.41\,\text{nC}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Dark period.
\series default
 From 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{inc}$
\end_inset
 on there is no new ionization: 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{cs}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
 stay fixed, and the cloud drifts as a rigid body according to the paper's Eq. (6), now with a time-dependent applied field: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{dz_{\text{drift}}}{dt}=b\left[E_{ex}\left(z_{c},t\right)+E_{I,rod}\left(z_{c}\right)+E_{I,plane}\left(z_{c}\right)\right],\qquad z_{c}(t)=z_{c,0}+z_{\text{drift}}(t),\label{eq:drift_impulse}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

 where the image fields are those of the drifted cloud (rod and plane fixed).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Applied field at every instant.
\series default
 Laplace's equation is linear and the charge simulation method solves a linear system 
\begin_inset Formula $Pq=V$
\end_inset
, so the applied field is exactly proportional to the voltage: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{ex}(z,t)=V(t)\,e_{ex}(z),\label{eq:linearity}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{ex}(z)$
\end_inset
 is the applied field for 
\begin_inset Formula $1\,\text{V}$
\end_inset
. It is computed once and scaled, instead of re-solving the charge simulation at every time step.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Fields at each time step.
\series default
 For Figure 5(a) the result is 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{ex}(z,t)$
\end_inset
. For Figure 5(b) it is 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{ex}$
\end_inset
 before 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{inc}$
\end_inset
, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{ex}+E_{sp}+E_{I,rod}+E_{I,plane}$
\end_inset
 with the drifted cloud after it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Second-streamer criterion.
\series default
 A second streamer is assumed to incept when the total field at the tip returns to the applied field at the tip at the first inception, 
\begin_inset Formula $149.37\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
. The instant is found by root finding; the second streamer itself is not modelled.
\end_layout

