\begin_layout Subsection
The Impulse Tail
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The paper gives only the crest and the front. A double exponential needs two time constants, so a tail must be assumed. We use the standard switching-impulse tail of IEC 60060-1 (
\begin_inset Formula $T_{h}=2500\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
), read the paper's “time of wavefront” as the time to crest (the IEC definition for switching impulses), and keep 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{h}$
\end_inset
 as an input. In the first microseconds the tail still matters, because it changes 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset
 through the fit (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:tail"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
). This uncertainty shifts the inception instant by about 
\begin_inset Formula $0.5\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
 but does not change the conclusions.
\end_layout

