\begin_layout Subsection
Reading the Time Axis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At first sight a 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $4\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
 axis looks too short for a 
\begin_inset Formula $100\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
 front. With the fitted waveform, however, 
\begin_inset Formula $V(t)$
\end_inset
 passes 
\begin_inset Formula $27.5\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $t\approx1.5$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $2.1\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
, i.e. 
\series bold
inside
\series default
 the plotted window. We therefore take 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
 as the time from the start of the impulse: the early curves show the field before inception (identical in (a) and (b)), and the later ones show the space charge distorting it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Why the Drift Is Included
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The paper calculates Figure 5(b) under “the same condition as Figure 4”, which is the drift figure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
During the dark period the charge is frozen but the voltage keeps rising. The drift speed 
\begin_inset Formula $b\left[E_{ex}+E_{I}\right]$
\end_inset
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c}$
\end_inset
 therefore grows with time, so drift and rising voltage are coupled and cannot be treated separately.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The drift is small (about 
\begin_inset Formula $0.026\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $4\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
) but it acts on the field near the tip, which decides when the next streamer starts (see Results).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Second-Streamer Criterion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The paper gives the first inception as a 
\series bold
voltage
\series default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $27.5\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
, which the authors also checked experimentally. It does not give a field. At that instant no space charge exists yet, so the tip field is the applied field alone. With our CSM model for 
\begin_inset Formula $R=0.1\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 this is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{inc}=E_{ex}\left(0\right)\big|_{V=27.5\,\text{kV}}=149.37\,\text{kV/cm}.\label{eq:inception_field}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This field is a property of the electrode and the air, not of the voltage waveform. The tip geometry and the gas do not change after the first streamer, so the next streamer needs the same tip field again. The difference is that the tip field is now the sum of the applied field and the fields of the residual charge:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{total}(0,t)=V(t)\,e_{ex}(0)+E_{sp}\left(0;z_{\text{drift}}(t)\right)+E_{I,rod}\left(0;z_{\text{drift}}(t)\right)+E_{I,plane}\left(0;z_{\text{drift}}(t)\right)\;\ge\;E_{inc}.\label{eq:second_criterion}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The value 
\begin_inset Formula $149.37\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 is therefore our number, derived from the paper's voltage. A different applied-field model would give a slightly different threshold, but the idea stays the same: the voltage the paper gives is turned into a tip field once, and that field becomes the trigger.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The sequence is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The first streamer forms at 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{inc}=1.671\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
. It lasts a very short time; what it leaves behind is the cloud of positive ions (the cone of space charge). This cloud shields the tip and drops the tip field from 
\begin_inset Formula $149.37$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $-3.6\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
During the dark period the cloud drifts slowly towards the plane (it is the residual charge that drifts, not the streamer). At the same time the impulse voltage keeps rising.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The tip field recovers for 
\series bold
both
\series default
 reasons together: the rising voltage increases the applied field, and the receding cloud shields the tip less.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The first instant at which 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:second_criterion"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 holds is found by root finding. We record both the time and the voltage at that instant.
\end_layout

