\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Inception, dark period and the second-streamer criterion under the 320 kV, 100/2500 µs impulse.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "tab:cycle"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second streamer therefore needs about 
\begin_inset Formula $51\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
 instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $27.5\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
: the charge left by the first streamer raises the voltage needed for the next one. This is the space-charge effect the paper studies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Why not the critical field of 5 kV/cm.
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}=5\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 is a different quantity. It is the streamer 
\series bold
propagation
\series default
 (stability) field: the average field a positive streamer needs along its path to keep growing. That is why it is applied at the streamer head, 
\begin_inset Formula $z=L_{cs}$
\end_inset
, to fix 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
. Starting a new streamer at the tip needs ionization, which in air requires roughly 
\begin_inset Formula $25$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $30\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
. A threshold of 
\begin_inset Formula $5\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 at the tip would be met a fraction of a microsecond after the first streamer and would give an unphysical burst of streamers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Limitation.
\series default
 This criterion looks at the tip field only. A stricter criterion is the ionization integral along the axis, 
\begin_inset Formula $\int\left(\alpha-\eta\right)dz\ge K$
\end_inset
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $K\approx9$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $18$
\end_inset
, which also accounts for how the space charge flattens the field just in front of the tip. Because the tip-field rule ignores that shape, it probably gives a slightly early estimate. This is acceptable here because only the instant is reported and the second streamer is not modelled.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Why the Second Streamer Is Not Modelled
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once the tip field recovers, the paper's model would create a second streamer with its own space charge. Modelling it requires its length and charge, which depend on the field at that moment, and it goes beyond what Figure 5 shows. The simulation therefore only reports when the criterion is met. Curves after that instant show the field as if no second streamer had formed and are labelled accordingly in the output.
\end_layout

